-The implementation must prevent two concurrent attendants from successfully
-calling the same waiting ticket. The exact locking strategy belongs to the
-implementation and database transaction layer; the domain invariant is that a
-ticket can be called only once.
-
-> TODO (follow-up from review): link the test covering the single-call
-> invariant and concurrent-call protection once implemented.
+Implemented by `App\Actions\CallNextTicket` (`CallMode::Any`,
+`PriorityOnly`, `NormalOnly`; rules in [`business-rules.md`](business-rules.md)).
+Everything runs in one database transaction:
+
+1. Lock the queue row (`lockForUpdate`) and read `is_active` from it, not from
+   the passed model; an inactive queue throws `InactiveQueueException`.
+2. Pick the candidate: waiting tickets of the queue ordered by `priority` desc,
+   `issued_at` asc, `id` asc, restricted by mode and by the fairness rule
+   (last 3 calls of the queue, ordered by `called_at` desc, `id` desc).
+   No eligible ticket throws `NoWaitingTicketsException`.
+3. Lock the candidate row and re-check it is still `waiting`. `Ticket::call()`
+   locks only the ticket, not the queue, so a candidate can be called
+   concurrently; if so it is skipped and the next one is picked.
+4. Set `status=called`, `called_at` and `called_by_user_id` (the only fields
+   the ticket write-once rule allows) and return the updated ticket.
+
+The action does no authorization; the caller must have authorized the attendant.
+`lockForUpdate()` is a no-op on SQLite (tests), where the status re-check is
+the safety net. Tests: `tests/Feature/CallNextTicketTest.php`.
 
